let html=`<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Why triangle angles total 180° — pending teacher review</title><style>@page{size:A4;margin:18mm}body{font:11pt/1.4 Arial,sans-serif;color:#182638}section{break-before:page}section:first-child{break-before:auto}h1{font-size:25pt;color:#324e7a}h2{font-size:20pt}h3{font-size:15pt}svg{display:block;max-width:310px;max-height:280px;margin:12px auto}code{font:9pt monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.status{padding:12px;border:2px solid #a27322;background:#fff7df;font-weight:bold}.math{background:#f0ecfc;padding:14px;line-height:1.8}li{margin-bottom:5px}.answer{border-left:4px solid #687ca6;padding-left:15px}p{orphans:3;widows:3}</style><body>`;
html+=`<section><p class="status">PENDING EDUCATOR REVIEW — no approval or release is claimed</p><h1>Why triangle angles total 180°</h1><h2>Learner pages and separate teacher notes</h2><p>Prepared for Prem · 9 November 2026. A bounded triangle lesson, using the same explanation text, examples and question IDs as the implemented learner flow.</p><p>Prior knowledge: recognise triangle sides and corners; add and subtract whole-number degrees. The lesson explains an interior angle, a straight angle and the parallel-line reasoning it uses. A teacher should check the learner understands alternate interior angles.</p><p>The interactive version lets learners move C by touch or labelled keyboard controls. Measurements are approximate; the parallel-line argument supplies the reason. The static diagrams here use the same default 30°/90°/60° geometry. Flat positions are rejected.</p>${diagram('static-diagram.svg')}<p>Eight revised calculus explanations from the previous milestone remain unchanged. This packet concentrates on the new geometry lesson. Answer keys and review material start after the learner practice page.</p></section>`;
